The setup is pre-wired in `main.tsx` with fonts.css + styles.css + ThemeProvider (theme="light"). I need to make the theme dynamic — move theme state up. I'll restructure:

- `main.tsx`: keep CSS imports; render `<App />` — but ThemeProvider needs the theme state. Better: keep main.tsx as the entry but lift theme state into a component. I can create `Root` component in App.tsx that owns theme state and renders `<ThemeProvider theme={theme}>`. But main.tsx already wraps App in `<ThemeProvider theme="light">`. If I nest another ThemeProvider inside, the inner one becomes scoped (per the trap doc: nested provider is always scoped) — that would break the global body class switching. So I must edit main.tsx to not fix the theme, or move ThemeProvider into App.

Cleanest: edit main.tsx to render `<App />` without ThemeProvider, and put ThemeProvider inside App with state. Or keep main.tsx structure and pass theme via a small wrapper. I'll edit main.tsx: remove the static ThemeProvider, and in App.tsx own `theme` state + `ThemeProvider`.

Actually simpler and still clean: keep ThemeProvider in main.tsx but make it a stateful `Root` component defined in App.tsx. Let me just modify main.tsx minimally:

```tsx
import '@gravity-ui/uikit/styles/fonts.css';
